\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Geometry of the native-launch test. Left: the three source circles have distinct centers; their horizontal projections are radial segments because their planes are vertical. The arrows are the fixed oriented normals, not signs of the launched means. Right: the actual receiving circle lies in the xz plane with increasing theta clockwise in this view, normal \(+e_y\), and \(b=wR_\mitGamma \). The long R separation is broken schematically; the picture is not to scale. The body center is displaced by \(wK_c\) from the circle center.}}{9}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:native}{{2}{9}{Geometry of the native-launch test. Left: the three source circles have distinct centers; their horizontal projections are radial segments because their planes are vertical. The arrows are the fixed oriented normals, not signs of the launched means. Right: the actual receiving circle lies in the xz plane with increasing theta clockwise in this view, normal \(+\ey \), and \(b=wR_\Gamma \). The long R separation is broken schematically; the picture is not to scale. The body center is displaced by \(wK_c\) from the circle center}{figure.2}{}}
